\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Moderator-conditional functional error on the three regimes eligible for the transfer rules. \texttt  {grid\_skewness} and \texttt  {grid\_upper\_tail\_mean} are excluded from every training manifest, so PTA-S is marked \emph  {n/a} there: that is the transfer test, not a missing measurement. On D6 the true skewness effect is numerically zero, which is why that panel's bars vanish for every method. Numbers in Table~\ref {tab:all-tcate}.}}{18}{figure.caption.16}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:functionals}{{6}{18}{Moderator-conditional functional error on the three regimes eligible for the transfer rules. \texttt {grid\_skewness} and \texttt {grid\_upper\_tail\_mean} are excluded from every training manifest, so PTA-S is marked \emph {n/a} there: that is the transfer test, not a missing measurement. On D6 the true skewness effect is numerically zero, which is why that panel's bars vanish for every method. Numbers in Table~\ref {tab:all-tcate}}{figure.caption.16}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces What each contrast rule costs and buys, on the grid causal mean. D2 is the null regime the gate failed on and the dashed line is its preregistered cap. D0 is exactly recoverable, so a rule that cannot switch itself off is penalised there, which is what happens to the hand-tuned fixed ridge. D3 is built to favour separate heads, so a rule that shrinks every contrast is penalised there, which is what happens to both fixed rules. Only the cross-fitted rule is acceptable in all three.}}{18}{figure.caption.17}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:contrastrules}{{7}{18}{What each contrast rule costs and buys, on the grid causal mean. D2 is the null regime the gate failed on and the dashed line is its preregistered cap. D0 is exactly recoverable, so a rule that cannot switch itself off is penalised there, which is what happens to the hand-tuned fixed ridge. D3 is built to favour separate heads, so a rule that shrinks every contrast is penalised there, which is what happens to both fixed rules. Only the cross-fitted rule is acceptable in all three}{figure.caption.17}{}}
